ing gradually as she is able to\nmove with minimal assistance. Therefore, she is being discharged today with a home nursing assistant.\nApart from that, her medication list includes warfarin, Feratab, paracetamol and oxycosone if required.       Commented [jc3]: oxycodone\n\nGiven the previous data, I would be grateful if you could follow-up Ms Johnson and monitor her FBE and\nINR. For any queries, please contact me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nDoctor\n\n\n\n\n                                     Report\n Word length                  203\n Comments                     This is an excellent letter with appropriate\n                              selection of case notes and logical paragraphing.\n                              There is good coherence throughout. There are\n                              hardly any inaccuracies and the letter meets the\n                              expectations.\n Estimated Grade               A\n Advice                       1. Always proofread your letter\n                              2. Keep up the good work!\n Helpful links                https://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-reading-\n                              practice-tests\n                              https://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-\n                              correction\n                              https://www.facebook.com/groups/oethelp\n\n\n\n\n296 Henley Beach Rd, Underdale, 5032\nhttp://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-correction\nwww.benchmarkedu.com.au/pteoetielts", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Ticket Id: 7427\n\nDr Tony Jones\nPrivate Practice\n12 New Street\nStillwater\n\n21/03/2015\n\nDear Dr Jones,\n\nRe: Ms Betty Johnson, 81 years of age\n\nI am writing to update you regarding your patient, Ms Johnson, who is being discharged today after a\nsuccessful recovery from total knee replacement surgery. Your further follow-up would be highly\nappreciated.\n\nOn 25/02/2015, the surgery was performed and Ms Johnson's condition postoperatively was stable.\nHowever, she had developed a severe anemia; for which, an intra-venous blood transfusion was done.\nAfter controlling her Hb level, Feratab was commenced. In terms of her medications, five days\npreoperatively, warfarin was ceased and was replaced by clexane. The later was ceased when her INR\nbecame elevated and warfarin was restarted again three days postoperatively. Regarding her wound, it has\nhealed well without oozing and clips have been removed since the surgery.\n\nOn 06/03/2015, Ms Johnson was transferred to a rehabilitation unit where her mobility improved\nsignificantly after doing gentle exercises. Now, her independence is increasing gradually as she is able to\nmove with minimal assistance. Therefore, she is being discharged today with a home nursing assistant.\nApart from that, her medication list includes warfarin, Feratab, paracetamol and oxycosone if required.\n\nGiven the previous data, I would be grateful if you could follow-up Ms Johnson and monitor her FBE and\nINR. For any queries, please contact me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nDoctor\n\nReport\n\nWord length\nComments\n\nEstimated Grade\nAdvice\n\nHelpful links\n\n203\nThis is an excellent letter with appropriate\nselection of case notes and logical paragraphing.\nThere is good coherence throughout. There are\nhardly any inaccuracies and the letter meets the\nexpectations.\nA\n1. Always proofread your letter\n2. Keep up the good work!\nhttps://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-reading-\npractice-tests\nhttps://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-\ncorrection\nhttps://www.facebook.com/groups/oethelp\n\n296 Henley Beach Rd, Underdale, 5032\nhttp://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-correction\nwww.benchmarkedu.com.au/pteoetielts\n\nCommented [jc1]: Clexane\n\nCommented [jc2]: latter\n\nCommented [jc3]: oxycodone", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 841.92, "height": 1190.64, "blocks": [{"text": "Ticket Id: 7427", "lines": [{"text": "Ticket Id: 7427", "x": 0.47089984796655265, "y":